<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>1.7. Gauss' Law and the Divergence Theorem</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-gauss-law">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Gauss' Law</term>. Consider an electric field <m>\mbf E</m> through a closed surface <m>S</m>
        which encloses the total charge <m>Q</m>. 
      
        Then the electric 
        <em>flux</em> out of <m>S</m> is equal to
        the total charge enclosed scaled by the permittivity constant, that is
        <md>
          \ds \text{flux}  = \oiint_S \mbf E \cdot ~d\mbf A = \oiint_S \mbf E \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA= \frac{Q}{\epsilon_0}
        </md>
        where  <m>\epsilon_0</m> is the <em>free space permittivity</em> constant (<m>\epsilon_0\approx 8.854\times 10^{-12}</m> Farads/meter) and 
        <m>\hat n</m> is the <em>outward normal vector</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The electric displacement, <m>\mbf D</m>, is given by <m>\mbf D = \epsilon_0 \mbf E</m>, thus equivalently 
        <md>
          \oiint_S \mbf D \cdot ~d\mbf A = \oiint_S \mbf D \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA = Q.
        </md>
        Using the divergence theorem where <m>\rho</m> is the total volume charge density (charge per unit volume), the <term>differential form of Gauss' Law</term> is:
        <md>
          \ds \nabla \cdot \mbf E = \frac{\rho}{\epsilon_0}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
        To find the <term>electric potential <m>\phi</m></term>, choose a path <m>C</m> from an arbitrary basepoint <m>*</m> 
       to some point <m>(x,y)</m>. Then <md>\phi(x,y)= \work{C}{E}</md>.
      </p>
  

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have an infinitely thin charged plate whose
  charge density is 13 Coulombs per square meter as shown on the
  left below.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the symmetry of this situation imply about the
  direction of the electric field and electric displacement vectors?</p>
             <image width="45%" margins="0% 55%">
      <shortdescription>Arrows indicate the <m>x,y,z</m>-axes. A plane shown on the left in the <m>x,y</m>-plane, and on the right is a copy of the plane with a cylinder oriented such that the plane cuts the cylinder exactly in half and so that the plane makes a circular slice through the cylinder.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-8-gauss1">
        <diagram dimensions="(350,100)" margins="5">
          <definition>points=((0.6,0.4),(4.8,0.4),(5.8,2.4),(1.6,2.4))</definition>
    <definition>points2=((6.2,0.4),(10.4,0.4),(11.4,2.4),(7.2,2.4))</definition>
    <definition>f(x)=-0.1*sqrt(1 - ((x-8.8)/(0.8))^2)+2.1</definition>
    <definition>g(x)=-0.1*sqrt(1 - ((x-8.8)/(0.8))^2)+1.45</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(0,0,12,3)">
            <axes  decorations="no" axes="none"/>
            <polygon points="points" stroke="black" closed="yes" fill="gray" fill-opacity="0.2"/>
            <polygon points="points2" stroke="black" closed="yes" fill="gray" fill-opacity="0.2"/>
            <path start="(8.0,2.1)" stroke="none" closed="yes" fill="white">
              <lineto point="(8.0,1.45)" />
              <graph function="g" domain="(8,9.6)"/>
              <lineto point="(9.6,2.1)" />
              <graph function="f" />
            </path>
            <ellipse center="(8.8,2.1)" axes="(.8,0.1)" stroke="blue" rotate="0" degrees="no" fill="white"/>
            <ellipse center="(8.8,.8)" axes="(.8,0.1)" stroke="blue" rotate="0" degrees="no" dash="2.3"  opacity="0.4"/>
            <ellipse center="(8.8,1.45)" axes="(.8,0.1)" stroke="blue" rotate="0" degrees="no" dash="2.3" fill="gray" opacity="0.4"/>
            <line p1="(8.0,2.1)" p2="(8.0,1.45)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line p1="(9.6,2.1)" p2="(9.6,1.45)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line p1="(8.0,.8)" p2="(8.0,1.45)" stroke="blue" dash="2.3" opacity="0.4"/>
            <line p1="(9.6,.8)" p2="(9.6,1.45)" stroke="blue" dash="2.3" opacity="0.4"/>
            <vector v="(1,0)" tail="(0.9,0.6)" thickness="2"/>
            <vector v="(0,1)" tail="(0.9,0.6)" thickness="2"/>
            <vector v="(1/sqrt(5),2/sqrt(5))" tail="(0.9,0.6)" thickness="2"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose we take a Gaussian surface that is a cylinder of radius <m>r</m>
  as shown on the right above.  How much electric charge is contained within
  the cylinder?  (Remember that all the charge is on the plate.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does Gauss' Law tell us about the strength of the electric
  displacement <m>\mbf D</m> at a position <m>h</m> meters above the plate?
  (What can you say about the flux of <m>\mbf D</m> through the curvy sides
  of the cylinder?)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that we want to create a potential <m>\phi</m> for the electric
  field <m>\mbf E</m> (remember that <m>\mbf D = \epsilon_0 \mbf E</m>).  Let's
  choose a basepoint <m>*</m> on the plate.  What is the value of the
  potential <m>h</m> meters above the plate?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have an infinitely thin, infinitely long charged
  wire whose charge density is <m>\lambda</m> Coulombs per meter.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Choose a
  cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m> as shown
  below. How much electric charge is inside the surface?</p>
             <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
      <shortdescription>Arrows indicate the <m>x,y,z</m>-axes. A wire extends vertically, and a cylinder gaussian surface is wrapped symmetrically around the wire.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-8-gauss2">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
          <coordinates bbox="(7,1,10.5,2.5)">
            <axes  decorations="no" axes="none"/>
            <definition>f(x)=-0.1*sqrt(1 - ((x-8.8)/(0.8))^2)+1.45</definition>
            <ellipse center="(8.8,2.1)" axes="(.8,0.1)" stroke="blue" rotate="0" degrees="no" fill="white"/>
            <ellipse center="(8.8,1.45)" axes="(.8,0.1)" stroke="blue" rotate="0" degrees="no" dash="2.3" fill="gray" opacity="0.4"/>

            <line p1="(8.0,2.1)" p2="(8.0,1.45)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line p1="(9.6,2.1)" p2="(9.6,1.45)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line p1="(8.8,1)" p2="(8.8,1.34)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line p1="(8.8,1.34)" p2="(8.8,2)" stroke="blue" dash="2" opacity="0.8"/>
            <line p1="(8.8,2)" p2="(8.8,2.5)" stroke="blue"/>
            <path start="(8,1.45)" stroke="blue" closed="no">
              <graph function="f" domain="(8.0,9.6)"/>
            </path>
            <vector v="(0.4,0)" tail="(7.5,1.45)" thickness="2"/>
            <vector v="(0,0.4)" tail="(7.5,1.45)" thickness="2"/>
            <vector v="(2*0.4/sqrt(5),1*0.4/sqrt(5))" tail="(7.5,1.45)" thickness="2"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the symmetry of the situation say about the direction of
  the electric field and electric displacement?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use Gauss' Law to find the electric displacement and electric field
  a distance <m>r</m> from the wire.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose we choose a basepoint <m>1</m> meter away from the wire.  Find
  the electric potential <m>r</m> meters away from the wire.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have a fluid whose velocity is given by the
  field <m>\mbf F(x,y,z)</m> meters per second.  Suppose that the density
  of the fluid is <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> kilograms per meter<m>^3</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>E</m> is a three-dimensional region, what is the physical meaning
  of <m>\iiint_E \rho~dV</m>?  (Thinking about units will help.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>\mbf U = \rho \mbf F</m>.  What are the units on <m>U</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>S</m> is a surface, what is the physical meaning of <m>\ds \iint_S \mbf
  U \cdot \hat\nvec~ dA</m> if <m>\hat \nvec</m> is the outward normal to <m>S</m>? (Think units again...)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>S</m> is a closed surface with outward normal <m>\hat \nvec</m> bounding a
  region <m>E</m>, explain why 
  <md>
  \ds \frac{d}{dt}\iiint_E \rho ~dV = -\oiint_S \mbf U\cdot\hat \nvec~dA,
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why <m>\ds \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot \mbf
  U = 0</m>.   This is called the <term>continuity equation</term> which describes the transport of mass and is an
  expression of conservation of mass.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Fun fact: <term>Continuity equations</term> are stronger, local versions of conservation laws.  For example, the 
    <term>Law of Conservation of Mass</term> says that matter cannot be created or destroyed in an isolated system, 
    that is, the total mass is conserved. But it does not say that matter cannot teleport!  Matter that disappears
    from one area and reappears instantaneously in another area within the system would be completely acceptable 
    according to the conservation law. The continuity 
    equation says more: mass is <em>locally</em> conserved. Matter cannot be created nor destroyed, and it also cannot teleport. It can only move 
    through a continuous flow. The way that mass flows is described by its flux. (Other quantities with continuity equations include energy, momentum, electric charge, ...)
  </p>
</worksheet>
